<BlogPost slug="how-to-detox-from-weed" toc={toc}>
  <p>More than half of Americans have used weed at some point in their lives [1]. While many people smoke or ingest it recreationally or for medical purposes, weed is an addictive substance, and cannabis use disorder (CUD) can be a real consequence of using it. Around 3 in 10 people who use cannabis have CUD [2].</p>
  <p>Weed addiction, like most addictions, can lead to withdrawal symptoms when use is suddenly stopped or dramatically lowered after a long period. Detoxing from weed not only helps a person safely remove weed from their system, but also helps them manage withdrawal and move forward into further treatment.</p>
  <p>Let&rsquo;s look at how to effectively detox from weed, and the structured support it requires.</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/cud-prevalence-dial.svg" alt="About 3 in 10 people who use cannabis in the U.S. meet criteria for cannabis use disorder" loading="lazy" width="760" height="320" />
    <figcaption>Cannabis use disorder is common among people who use cannabis regularly.</figcaption>
