  <h2 id="what-is-cud">What Is Cannabis Use Disorder?</h2>
  <p>CUD is a mental health condition and substance use disorder (SUD). People experiencing CUD cannot control cravings for cannabis or stop using despite the negative effects on their lives.</p>
  <p>The condition applies to any product that comes from the cannabis sativa plant, including dried flowers, oils, gummies, and vapes. Most CUD, however, is tied to marijuana, which contains tetrahydrocannabinol (THC), the chemical compound that causes the &ldquo;high&rdquo; feeling.</p>
  <p>Signs of CUD include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Cravings for cannabis</li>
    <li>Using more cannabis, and for more time, than intended</li>
    <li>Unsuccessfully cutting back or stopping cannabis use</li>
    <li>Spending time and money getting cannabis</li>
    <li>Spending time recovering from using cannabis</li>
    <li>Struggling at work, school, in relationships, and with other responsibilities because of cannabis use</li>
    <li>Giving up hobbies and activities you enjoy to use cannabis instead</li>
    <li>Needing higher doses of cannabis to achieve the same effects as before</li>
    <li>Experiencing withdrawal symptoms when stopping or lowering cannabis use</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="causes">What Causes CUD?</h3>
  <p>There&rsquo;s no single cause of CUD. It usually develops from a mix of factors. People in adolescence, and people with existing mental health conditions, are most vulnerable to developing it [3].</p>
  <p>Common causes of CUD include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Biological:</b> THC interacts with the brain&rsquo;s reward system and drives reward-seeking behavior.</li>
    <li><b>Genetic:</b> CUD can run in families; stress, social pressure, and easy access to cannabis all add to that inherited risk.</li>
    <li><b>Environmental:</b> Trauma, especially childhood trauma, and socioeconomic hardship can also raise the risk.</li>
    <li><b>Psychiatric:</b> CUD often co-occurs with <a href="/blog/what-is-dual-diagnosis/">depression, anxiety, PTSD, and bipolar disorder</a>.</li>
  </ul>

