  <h2 id="withdrawal-symptoms">What Are Weed Withdrawal Symptoms?</h2>
  <p>Around half of people who use weed experience withdrawal when they stop [4]. Withdrawal is what happens when the body has to adjust to the absence of weed.</p>
  <p>Withdrawal symptoms include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Anxiety</li>
    <li>Irritability</li>
    <li>Aggression</li>
    <li>Trouble sleeping</li>
    <li>Low mood</li>
    <li>Loss of appetite</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Other people report chills, headaches, tension, sweating, and stomach pain during withdrawal [4].</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/weed-withdrawal-symptoms.svg" alt="About half of people who use weed experience withdrawal, with common symptoms including anxiety, irritability, aggression, trouble sleeping, low mood, and loss of appetite, plus chills, headaches, tension, sweating, and stomach pain" loading="lazy" width="760" height="420" />
    <figcaption>Withdrawal isn&rsquo;t life-threatening, but it can be hard to manage without support.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="on-my-own">Can I Detox From Weed On My Own?</h2>
  <p>It&rsquo;s possible to detox from weed at home alone, but it&rsquo;s not recommended. Detoxing without support can bring risks that hinder recovery and put a person in danger:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Withdrawal symptoms may persist for weeks and be hard to manage at home</li>
    <li>A person could hurt themselves while in a vulnerable state alone</li>
    <li>The mental health support that&rsquo;s needed may not be immediately available</li>
    <li>The risk of relapse is higher when detoxing alone</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Medical detox, on the other hand, provides a safe, regulated space to withdraw from weed. Treating an addiction requires working through the trauma that often lies beneath physical dependency, and medical detox offers a solid foundation for that work.</p>

  <h2 id="medical-detox">What Is Medical Detox for Weed?</h2>
  <p><a href="/programs/medical-detox/">Medical detox</a> is a structured form of care for anyone removing addictive substances from their system. It involves staying at a health center under 24/7 supervision, where staff help manage cravings and withdrawal symptoms, and help people find stability without marijuana.</p>
  <p>Anyone with CUD can attend medical detox, whether it&rsquo;s their first time or they&rsquo;ve tried before and weren&rsquo;t able to finish. All medical detox programs are personalized, factoring in medical history, present needs, and goals for recovery.</p>
  <p>Medical detox for weed is structured in three main parts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Assessment:</b> The detox team conducts a physical and mental health evaluation.</li>
    <li><b>Stabilization:</b> The person joins therapy and wellness activities and takes medications, when prescribed by the clinical team, to keep cravings and withdrawal symptoms in check [5].</li>
    <li><b>Preparation:</b> The detox team recommends next steps&mdash;such as an outpatient program or weekly therapy&mdash;to maintain progress and abstain from cannabis.</li>
  </ul>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/weed-medical-detox-steps.svg" alt="Medical detox for weed has three main parts: assessment, a physical and mental health evaluation; stabilization, therapy, wellness activities, and medication when prescribed; and preparation, a plan for ongoing care after detox" loading="lazy" width="760" height="320" />
    <figcaption>Medical detox for weed typically lasts 3 to 10 days, depending on use history and co-occurring conditions.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Medical detox lasts 3 to 10 days, on average. The duration depends on how much weed a person uses, how often, and whether they have other co-occurring disorders.</p>
  <p>Unlike detoxing at home, medical detox combines evidence-based approaches such as Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) with activities that build better emotional regulation and skills for a healthy, drug-free life. These therapies also help people examine the thoughts and behaviors that drive their relationship with weed.</p>

